W G8 (invasion), B F6, W H7, B F8, W G7, B H9 (placement), W G9, B J9, W H8, B J6, W J7, B F7,
W H6, B J5, W J8 (captures 2), B F9 => my 8-stone group had ONE 2-point eye (H9/J9): dead.
W C7 (2nd invasion), B C8, W B8, B B7, W D8, B C9, W B9, B C6, W D9, B C7, W E8 (vital point),
B A4, W A3, B A5, W J3, B J4, W J1, B J3, W H1, B B3!, W C3, B B2, W C2, B B1, W C1, B A2
(captures A3), W A7, B A8, W A9, B A8 (captures 3), pass pass.
Takeaways:
- Opening as White was too LOW: C4, F4, G3, B4, D4 all 3rd/4th line. Black's G6/D7/B6/H4 framed
  the top AND both sides; my answers (B4 to B5, D4 to C5, E5/F5/G4 to D6/E6/G5) were all
  "follow the opponent" moves. Result was a clean rows-1-4 vs rows-5-9 split = Black wins by 10.
  As White, a clean 4-row bottom is NOT enough (need 38). Must contest the top EARLY (by
  move 6-8) while Black's top is still thin — e.g. W E7/F7 between D7 and G6 or a 3-3 at G8/C8
  before Black builds the D6-E6-F6 wall.
- Late invasion at G8 (3 rows deep, Black wall on rows 6+F col) had one eye only. Black H9
  placement + J6 hane + F7/F9 took both sides. Rule: an invasion behind a completed wall needs
  ~6 points of edge on BOTH sides; if the opponent can play the 1-2 point (H9) and the 2nd-line
  hane (J6) there is only one eye. Don't invade where the wall is already solid; invade earlier.
- Endgame: B A4 hane, W A3 atari (single stone, 2 libs) let B B3 ataris and take the whole
  A1-B3 corner (~6 pts). Answer an edge hane with the SOLID block (B3/A3 + connect) and keep
  the stone touching my wall; never leave a lone 2-lib stone on the 1st/2nd line.
- Tactic note: the huge Black group had only 4 libs at one point (A6,A7,E7,E9) and no eyes, but
  E7/E9 were shared with my 2-lib D8 group (= seki shape) and Black could always kill my one-eyed
  right group to gain libs. A one-eyed group of mine next to it is a lib source for them.

## Game 10 — White vs 14k — LOSS by resignation (move 46, was ~-25)
Moves: B F6, W D4, B F3, W D6, B C5 (wedge), W D5, B C4, W C6, B D3, W E7, B F7, W F5, B G5,
W F4, B G4, W E4, B E3 => my D4-D6/E4-F5 wall SANDWICHED (black C4/C5 left, D3-F3 below,
F6/G4 right). W F8, B G8, W E8, B B6, W B7, B A5, W H6 (invasion), B G6, W H7, B G7, W H8,
B G9, W H5, B H4, W H9, B J5!, W E6, B J4, W J6, B J8 (vital point) => right group H5-H9 dead.
W F9, B G2, W C3 (desperate cut), B B3, W C2, B B2, W D2, B E2 -> dead too. Resigned.
Takeaways:
- Opening D4+D6 vs F6/F3 was a "two stones on one line" shape; black wedged C5 behind it and
  then C4/D3 underneath = my stones became a center wall with no corner. My territory was only
  the top-left ~26 pts. ALWAYS keep a corner/edge base under my stones: answer B C5 with C4
  (block the bottom-left corner) or C3, not D5 (D5 just connected, black then took C4 & D3).
- EDGE EYE SPACE IS NOT 'STRAIGHT FOUR' WHEN THE END POINT TOUCHES A LIVING ENEMY STONE:
  H5-H9 wall with J6-J9 empty looked alive, but black J5 (connected to a strong group) makes J6
  a boundary point: real space = J7-J9 (straight three) and black J8 kills. Before calling a
  side group alive, count only points that the enemy CANNOT push into from outside. A 1-wide
  column group needs ~5 enclosed points or a second eye elsewhere. When black hane'd J5 I should
  have played J4/J5 myself earlier (H4 was the moment: W H4 instead of letting black H4).
- Invasion H6 was reasonable in timing (move 25) but I chose a 1-wide strip (H-col vs G-col
  black wall). Better invasion point with eye potential: a corner (H3/G3 area or H8 3-3).

